WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.097
This query starts from patients and left joins admissions.

00:00:03.097 --> 00:00:06.922
The patient table contributes one descriptive row per patient.

00:00:06.922 --> 00:00:09.637
Admissions provides the events being counted.

00:00:09.637 --> 00:00:14.467
Filtered counts create January, February, and March admission columns.

00:00:14.467 --> 00:00:17.942
The GROUP BY keeps one output row per patient.

00:00:17.942 --> 00:00:22.522
Because the join relationship is understood, the counts remain meaningful.

00:00:22.522 --> 00:00:28.187
Connect this idea to a machine-learning workflow converting wide measurements into tidy long data.

00:00:28.187 --> 00:00:33.062
Ask what could go wrong if the concept were misunderstood: could rows be duplicated,

00:00:33.062 --> 00:00:39.707
important records disappear, categories be interpreted incorrectly, or a report become difficult to reproduce?

00:00:39.707 --> 00:00:44.637
A strong analyst can explain both the SQL operation and the assumption behind it.

00:00:44.637 --> 00:00:49.680
After the example, summarize the idea in one plain-language sentence before moving on.
